---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import JcecPhase3Progress from '../../../../../../components/JcecPhase3Progress.astro';
import { phase3ThermalSites } from '../../../../../../data/jabberwockyPhase3';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout title="Mission 1: Read the Thermal Warning | Jabberwocky 2190" description="Phase 3 Mission 1: temperature, thermal energy, particle motion, expansion, contraction and changes of state." themeColor="#081722">
  <div class="mission-shell" id="phase3-mission1">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: base },
      { label: 'Grade 7 Science', href: `${base}courses/grade-7-science/` },
      { label: 'Jabberwocky', href: `${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/` },
      { label: 'Phase 3', href: `${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/phase-3/` },
      { label: 'Mission 1' }
    ]} />

    <JcecPhase3Progress active={1} compact={true} />

    <header class="mission-hero">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">PHASE 3 // MISSION 1 OF 5</p><h1>READ THE THERMAL <span>WARNING</span></h1><p class="question">What is temperature actually telling us?</p></div>
      <aside><span>YOU ARE LEARNING</span><strong>How temperature evidence connects to particle motion and changes in matter</strong><small>3 classes × 45 minutes</small></aside>
    </header>

    <section class="mission-at-a-glance">
      <div><small>WHERE AM I?</small><strong>Mission 1 of 5</strong></div>
      <div><small>WHAT DO I HAND IN?</small><strong>1 Thermal Risk Card + 1 reflection</strong></div>
      <div><small>WHY DOES IT MATTER?</small><strong>JCEC cannot design heating or cooling until it understands the temperature evidence.</strong></div>
    </section>

    <nav class="step-nav" aria-label="Mission 1 steps">
      <a href="#your-mission"><b>1</b><span>Your Mission</span></a><a href="#learn"><b>2</b><span>Learn</span></a><a href="#investigate"><b>3</b><span>Investigate</span></a><a href="#decide"><b>4</b><span>Decide</span></a><a href="#record"><b>5</b><span>Record It</span></a>
    </nav>

    <section class="simple-step" id="your-mission">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>1</span><div><p>CLASS 1</p><h2>Your Mission</h2></div></div>
      <div class="briefing"><p>JCEC's first extended habitat tests have returned temperature warnings. Some sites lose heat, some stay warm, and some change dramatically across the day or year. Your Thermal Survival team must read the evidence before anyone chooses heaters, coolers or insulation. Measure temperature accurately, explain what heating and cooling do to particles and matter, then identify the most important thermal condition humans must prepare for on your continent.</p></div>
      <div class="assignment-result" id="assignment-result" hidden><span id="assignment-icon"></span><div><small>YOUR PHASE 3 CONTINENT</small><strong id="assignment-name"></strong><p id="assignment-subtitle"></p></div><div class="challenge-badge"><small>THERMAL CHALLENGE</small><b id="assignment-challenge"></b></div></div>
      <div class="missing-assignment" id="missing-assignment" hidden><strong>Your Phase 3 posting is not saved on this device yet.</strong><p>Return to the Phase 3 Hub and choose the continent your teacher assigned.</p><a href={`${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/phase-3/`}>Get Phase 3 posting →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="learn">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>2</span><div><p>CLASS 1</p><h2>Learn the Science</h2></div></div>
      <p class="step-intro">Keep one story in mind: <strong>heating and cooling change particle motion, and matter responds.</strong></p>
      <div class="science-grid">
        <article><span>🌡️</span><strong>TEMPERATURE</strong><p>Tells us about the average motion, or average kinetic energy, of particles in a sample.</p></article>
        <article><span>⚡</span><strong>THERMAL ENERGY</strong><p>Energy associated with all of the particles in a sample. It is related to temperature, but it is not the same thing.</p></article>
        <article><span>↕️</span><strong>HEATING &amp; COOLING</strong><p>Heating increases particle motion. Cooling decreases particle motion.</p></article>
        <article><span>🧊</span><strong>MATTER RESPONDS</strong><p>Heating or cooling can cause expansion, contraction and changes of state.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="temperature-thermal-comparison">
        <div><small>SMALL HOT SAMPLE</small><strong>60°C</strong><p>Higher temperature: its particles are moving faster on average.</p></div><span>≠</span><div><small>LARGE WARM SAMPLE</small><strong>40°C</strong><p>Lower temperature, but because it contains many more particles it may have more total thermal energy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="comparison-note"><strong>Key idea:</strong> Temperature tells you about average particle motion. Total thermal energy also depends on how much matter is present and what that matter is.</p>

      <section class="thermometer-skill">
        <div><p class="mini-label">THERMOMETER SKILL</p><h3>Measure temperature like a scientist</h3></div>
        <ol><li><strong>Read the scale</strong> before measuring.</li><li><strong>Check the unit</strong> — use °C in this mission.</li><li><strong>Read at eye level</strong> when using a liquid thermometer.</li><li><strong>Wait for the reading to stabilize.</strong></li><li><strong>Record the number and unit</strong>, for example 21°C.</li><li><strong>Handle the thermometer safely</strong> and follow teacher directions.</li></ol>
      </section>

      <details class="help-box"><summary>Need Help? Particle model in simple language</summary><div><p>Matter is made of tiny particles. The particles are always moving.</p><p>When matter is heated, its particles generally move faster. When it cools, they generally move more slowly.</p><p>Faster particle motion can change spacing between particles or allow a substance to change state. You do not need equations for this mission.</p><p><strong>One extra word:</strong> heat is thermal energy being transferred because there is a temperature difference.</p></div></details>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="investigate">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>3</span><div><p>CLASSES 1–2</p><h2>Investigate</h2></div></div>
      <section class="investigation-block">
        <div class="block-title"><div><small>MAIN INVESTIGATION</small><h3>Thermal Change Investigation</h3></div><span>TEAM WORK</span></div>
        <p><strong>Question:</strong> What evidence shows that temperature and matter change when thermal energy is transferred?</p>
        <div class="equipment-strip"><strong>Shared supplies:</strong><span>thermometers</span><span>reused cups/containers</span><span>room-temperature, cool and warm water</span><span>ice</span><span>bottle + balloon/flexible cover if available</span></div>
        <div class="safety-strip"><strong>Safety:</strong><span>No boiling water. Your teacher controls water temperature. Handle thermometers carefully.</span></div>

        <h4>Part A — Measure accurately</h4>
        <ol class="simple-procedure"><li>Look at the thermometer scale and identify the °C markings.</li><li>Measure the room-temperature water. Wait for the reading to stabilize and record it.</li><li>Repeat for cool water.</li><li>Repeat for warm water.</li><li>Compare the three readings. Write one sentence describing the pattern.</li></ol>
        <div class="data-preview"><div><small>SAMPLE</small><strong>Room-temperature water</strong><span>_____ °C</span></div><div><small>SAMPLE</small><strong>Cool water</strong><span>_____ °C</span></div><div><small>SAMPLE</small><strong>Warm water</strong><span>_____ °C</span></div></div>

        <h4>Part B — Matter responds</h4>
        <div class="observation-grid"><article><span>🧊</span><strong>State change</strong><p>Observe ice melting and/or water condensing. What changed? What happened to particle motion or arrangement?</p></article><article><span>🎈</span><strong>Expansion / contraction</strong><p>Observe air in a bottle with a balloon or flexible cover as it warms or cools. What changes in volume can you see?</p></article></div>

        <div class="teacher-evidence"><p class="mini-label">SHARED EVIDENCE — NO SPECIAL EQUIPMENT REQUIRED</p><div class="evidence-grid"><article><strong>Liquid expansion</strong><p>As the liquid in a liquid thermometer warms, it expands and rises in the narrow tube. Cooling causes it to contract and fall.</p></article><article><strong>Solid expansion</strong><p>Bridges and other long structures use expansion joints because solids can expand when heated and contract when cooled.</p></article></div></div>
        <div class="fallback-note"><strong>If equipment is limited:</strong><span>Rotate through shared thermometer stations. If an expansion demonstration is unavailable, use the observation/data cards above. The science evidence matters more than owning specialty apparatus.</span></div>
        <button type="button" class="small-button" id="print-phase3-mission1">Print Mission 1 investigation + Thermal Risk Card</button>
      </section>

      <section class="continent-case">
        <div class="block-title"><div><small>CLASS 3</small><h3>Your Continent Thermal Case</h3></div><span>3 CLUES ONLY</span></div>
        <p>Now apply the same science to your assigned continent. Use these three clues—not the old archives.</p>
        <div class="no-continent" id="no-continent"><strong>Phase 3 continent not found.</strong><a href={`${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/phase-3/`}>Return to Phase 3 Hub →</a></div>
        <div id="thermal-files" hidden>{phase3ThermalSites.map((site) => (
          <article class="thermal-file" data-thermal-file={site.id} hidden>
            <header><span>{site.icon}</span><div><small>{site.name} · {site.subtitle}</small><h3>{site.thermalChallenge}</h3></div></header>
            <div class="clue-grid">{site.mission1Clues.map((clue, index) => <article><b>{index + 1}</b><p>{clue}</p></article>)}</div>
            <div class="restriction"><small>EARLIER DEVELOPMENT RESTRICTION</small><p>{site.previousTeamThermalBriefing.developmentRestriction}</p></div>
          </article>
        ))}</div>
      </section>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="decide">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>4</span><div><p>CLASS 3</p><h2>Make a Decision</h2></div></div>
      <div class="decision-card"><p class="mini-label">ONE TEAM QUESTION</p><h3>What is the biggest thermal condition JCEC must prepare for on your continent?</h3><div class="option-row"><span>Losing heat too quickly</span><span>Gaining too much heat</span><span>Large temperature change</span><span>Prolonged cold</span></div><p class="decision-note">These are helpful categories, not answer keys. If your evidence supports another clearly stated thermal problem, defend it.</p><div class="reasoning-frame"><div><strong>EVIDENCE</strong><span>Our evidence shows ______.</span></div><div><strong>SCIENCE</strong><span>This matters because heating/cooling would cause ______.</span></div></div></div>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="record">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>5</span><div><p>CLASS 3</p><h2>Record It</h2></div></div>
      <div class="handoff-strip"><span>👥 <strong>TEAM WORK</strong> discuss</span><i>→</i><span>📝 <strong>TEAM RECORD</strong> one card</span><i>→</i><span>💭 <strong>MY REFLECTION</strong> everyone answers</span></div>
      <div class="team-record"><div class="record-heading"><div><p class="mini-label">TEAM RECORD</p><h3>Thermal Risk Card</h3></div><span>ONE PER TEAM</span></div><p>Keep only the evidence the next thermal mission may need.</p><div class="profile-checklist"><label><input type="checkbox" /> our continent</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> important temperature/environment evidence</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> biggest thermal problem</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> simple particle explanation</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> one physical effect of heating/cooling to watch</label></div></div>
      <div class="reflection-card"><p class="mini-label">MY REFLECTION</p><h3>Why is temperature useful evidence, but not the same thing as thermal energy?</h3><p>Everyone answers this question individually.</p></div>
      <div class="finished-box"><strong>Mission 1 is finished when:</strong><span>✓ your team has one Thermal Risk Card</span><span>✓ you completed the short individual reflection</span><p>Mission 2 — Follow the Heat — is coming next. Do not start it yet.</p><a href={`${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/phase-3/`}>Return to Phase 3 Mission Hub →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="print-packet" aria-label="Phase 3 Mission 1 printable packet">
      <section class="print-page"><h1>Phase 3 · Mission 1 — Thermal Change Investigation</h1><p>Team: ________________________________ &nbsp; Date: ____________</p><table><thead><tr><th>Sample</th><th>Temperature (°C)</th><th>What we notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Room-temperature water</th><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Cool water</th><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Warm water</th><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Pattern:</strong> __________________________________________________________________________</p><h2>Matter Responds</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Observation</th><th>What changed?</th><th>Particle explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Melting / condensation</th><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Expansion / contraction</th><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></section>
      <section class="print-page page-break"><h1>Thermal Risk Card</h1><p>Continent: ________________________________ &nbsp; Team: ________________________________</p><div class="print-grid two"><div><strong>Important temperature/environment evidence</strong></div><div><strong>Biggest thermal problem</strong></div></div><div class="print-grid two"><div><strong>Simple particle explanation</strong></div><div><strong>One physical effect to watch</strong></div></div><p><strong>Our evidence shows:</strong></p><div class="writing-lines"></div><p><strong>This matters because heating/cooling would cause:</strong></p><div class="writing-lines"></div><p><strong>MY REFLECTION:</strong> Why is temperature useful evidence, but not the same thing as thermal energy?</p><div class="writing-lines tall"></div></section>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script is:inline define:vars={{ siteData: phase3ThermalSites }}>
    (() => {
      const KEY = 'jabberwocky-phase3-posting';
      const sites = Object.fromEntries(siteData.map((site) => [site.id, site]));
      const saved = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
      const site = saved ? sites[saved] : null;
      const assignment = document.getElementById('assignment-result');
      const missing = document.getElementById('missing-assignment');
      const thermalFiles = document.getElementById('thermal-files');
      const noContinent = document.getElementById('no-continent');
      if (site) {
        assignment.hidden = false; missing.hidden = true; thermalFiles.hidden = false; noContinent.hidden = true;
        document.getElementById('assignment-icon').textContent = site.icon;
        document.getElementById('assignment-name').textContent = site.name;
        document.getElementById('assignment-subtitle').textContent = site.subtitle;
        document.getElementById('assignment-challenge').textContent = site.thermalChallenge;
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-thermal-file]').forEach((el) => { el.hidden = el.getAttribute('data-thermal-file') !== site.id; });
      } else {
        assignment.hidden = true; missing.hidden = false; thermalFiles.hidden = true; noContinent.hidden = false;
      }
      document.getElementById('print-phase3-mission1')?.addEventListener('click', () => window.print());
    })();
  </script>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
